          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem">What is a word counter and why do you need one?</h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem">A word counter is an online tool that instantly counts the number of words, characters, sentences and paragraphs in any text you paste or type. Whether you are a student checking your essay length, a writer meeting a publisher's word limit, a journalist hitting a column inch target or a social media manager fitting a caption into a character limit — a word counter saves you time and eliminates guesswork.</p>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">How many words should my text have?</h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem">The ideal word count depends entirely on the type of content and its purpose. Here are the most widely accepted guidelines:</p>
          <ul style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:2;padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem">
            <li><strong>Twitter / X:</strong> up to 280 characters per post</li>
            <li><strong>Instagram caption:</strong> up to 2,200 characters (recommended 125–150 for best engagement)</li>
            <li><strong>Email subject line:</strong> 40–60 characters to avoid being cut off</li>
            <li><strong>Short blog post:</strong> 300–600 words — good for news and quick tips</li>
            <li><strong>Standard blog post:</strong> 1,000–1,500 words — best for SEO and engagement</li>
            <li><strong>Long-form article:</strong> 2,000–3,500 words — ideal for ranking on Google</li>
            <li><strong>University essay:</strong> 1,500–3,000 words depending on level</li>
            <li><strong>Dissertation / thesis:</strong> 10,000–80,000 words depending on level</li>
            <li><strong>Short story:</strong> 1,000–7,500 words</li>
            <li><strong>Novel:</strong> 70,000–100,000 words</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">How long does it take to read or write X words?</h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem">The average adult reads at approximately 200–250 words per minute for non-fiction and 300 words per minute for fiction. Writing speeds vary more widely — from 250 to 500 words per hour for careful writing, up to 1,000+ words per hour for first drafts. Here are some useful reading time benchmarks:</p>
          <ul style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:2;padding-left:1.5rem;margin-bottom:1rem">
            <li><strong>500 words</strong> → approximately 2–3 minutes to read</li>
            <li><strong>1,000 words</strong> → approximately 4–5 minutes to read</li>
            <li><strong>1,500 words</strong> → approximately 6–8 minutes to read</li>
            <li><strong>5,000 words</strong> → approximately 20–25 minutes to read</li>
            <li><strong>10,000 words</strong> → approximately 40–50 minutes to read</li>
          </ul>

          <h2 style="font-family:var(--font-display);font-size:1.2rem;font-weight:700;margin-bottom:1rem;margin-top:1.25rem">Characters with and without spaces — what is the difference?</h2>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>Characters with spaces</strong> counts every character including spaces between words. This is the standard character count used by Twitter, SMS messages and most social media platforms.</p>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem"><strong>Characters without spaces</strong> counts only letters, numbers and punctuation marks, excluding all spaces. This count is used in academic citations, some publishing contracts and typographic calculations.</p>
          <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:0.9rem;line-height:1.8;margin-bottom:1rem">Our word counter shows both counts simultaneously so you always have the right number at hand, whatever format your platform requires.</p>

